      "name": null,
      "problem": "$3\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{27}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{27}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-\\sqrt{{x}^{5}} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{9\\cdot 3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-\\sqrt{{x}^{4}\\cdot x} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-\\sqrt{{x}^{4}}\\cdot \\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}-3\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{3}-3\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ (3-3)\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 0\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
